# How to add new l10n

Steps to add a new localisation to Craftify (MainApp, Share Extension, Common)

1. Prepare language code
   • Use the ISO-639-1 (or longer if needed) code, e.g. `zh-Hans`, `ja`.

2. Create resource folders
   ```
   src/Common/Resources/<code>.lproj/
   src/MainApp/Resources/<code>.lproj/
   src/ShareExtension/Resources/<code>.lproj/
   ```
   Each folder must contain a `Localizable.strings` file (UTF-8, no BOM).

3. Translate strings
   • Copy the English `.strings` file into each new folder.
   • Replace the values (right-hand side) with translated text.
   • Keep every key exactly as in the English file; do not add or delete keys.
   • Use `\"` to escape quotes and end every line with `;`.

4. Update `Project.swift` resource lists
   In the `resources` section of both targets, append the new folders:

   ```swift
   // MainApp target
   "src/MainApp/Resources/<code>.lproj/**",
   // ShareExtension target
   "src/ShareExtension/Resources/<code>.lproj/**",
   ```

5. (Optional) Extend language pickers
   • If the language should be selectable in UI drop-downs, ensure it exists in `SupportedLanguages.all` (Common/Sources/Models).
   • Add a new `SupportedLanguage` entry if it is missing.

6. Regenerate project & strings
   ```
   ./run generate
   ```

7. Validate
   ```
   ./run check
   ```
   All SwiftGen, lint, build & test stages must pass with zero warnings.

8. Test in the app
   • Run `./run sim`, switch system language to the new locale, open Craftify and the Share Extension.
   • Verify every screen shows translated text and no layout breaks.

9. Commit
   ```
   git add .
   git commit -m "Add <Language> localisation"
   ```

10. Update documentation (optional)
    • Mention the new language in `documents/architecture.md` or `README.md` if you keep a supported-languages list.

Following these steps keeps Craftify fully automated, linter-clean and CI/CD-friendly.

